= Flash memory — Technical =
'''Flash memory''' is nonvolatile semiconductor storage in which electrically modified charge states encode information. It is a device family, not a single storage protocol or guaranteed durability contract. This treatment separates cell physics, NAND/NOR topology, interface commands, controller algorithms and system-level persistence. DRAM refresh, flash erase, logical deletion and filesystem synchronization are different operations.

'''Reading depth:''' [[Flash memory/Simple|Simple]] · [[Flash memory/Technical|Technical]].

== Provenance and historical scope ==
Kioxia's account of its Toshiba predecessor places the flash-memory announcement in 1984 and NAND development in 1987, with a commercial 4-Mbit NAND product in 1991. These are manufacturer-documented milestones, not proof that all nonvolatile electrically erasable memory originated in one laboratory. Flash emerged from a broader EEPROM and semiconductor-memory research tradition.<ref name="history">Kioxia, [https://www.kioxia.com/en-jp/rd/technology/history.html ''Technology Development History''], corporate historical record and 1984/1987/1991 milestones.</ref>

== Charge-storage device physics ==
In common floating-gate devices, charge is stored on an electrically isolated conducting gate. In charge-trap variants, carriers occupy localized states in an insulating structure. The stored charge modifies the channel's effective threshold voltage; a sensing circuit infers a state from current under specified bias. Programming and erasure require controlled charge transfer through engineered insulating barriers. Field-assisted tunneling and other injection mechanisms depend on the actual cell design; neither a single voltage nor a single microscopic mechanism defines all flash products.

A useful first-order electrostatic relationship is <math>\Delta V_T\approx-\Delta Q/C_{\mathrm{eff}}</math> for a specified sign convention and effective coupling capacitance. It is a local lumped model, not a full floating-gate or charge-trap device equation: coupling to other electrodes, trapped-charge distribution, oxide fields and temperature matter. Reading involves threshold-voltage distributions, not an ideal binary switch.

For a purely illustrative calculation, suppose <math>|\Delta Q|=10^{-16}\ \mathrm C</math> and <math>C_{\mathrm{eff}}=2\times10^{-16}\ \mathrm F</math>. The magnitude is <math>|\Delta V_T|=0.5\ \mathrm V</math>. These invented parameters are not a measurement or a recommended programming target.

== NAND versus NOR topology ==
NAND strings place multiple cells in a series conduction path, allowing dense arrays with page-oriented data transfer. Readout depends on selected and pass-biased unselected cells, peripheral sense amplifiers and array organization. NOR's parallel access topology supports more directly addressable read behavior, useful for some execute-in-place firmware designs. Modern devices vary substantially in die stacking, cell structure, interface and controller features. In particular, planar and vertically stacked 3D NAND have distinct geometries and reliability mechanisms; conclusions about planar devices cannot automatically be transferred to 3D charge-trap strings.<ref name="spinelli2017">A. S. Spinelli, C. Monzio Compagnoni and A. L. Lacaita, [https://doi.org/10.3390/computers6020016 ''Reliability of NAND Flash Memories: Planar Cells and Emerging Issues in 3D Devices''], ''Computers'' 6(2), 16 (2017), scholarly review.</ref> Do not infer random byte programming, execute-in-place or fixed endurance from the NAND/NOR name alone.

