== Program, erase and command ordering ==
A raw NAND page program changes cell states according to allowed program sequences. Erasure resets a larger block before further programming. Reprogramming a page without erasure is generally restricted, and partial-page operations may have additional device-specific constraints. Page size, spare/OOB area, block geometry, plane/die organization, timing and legal command sequences must come from the exact part specification.

ONFI revision 6.0, dated 18 December 2025, is an authoritative interface document for its specified NAND protocol family. It must not be treated as a universal NAND implementation, NOR standard, SSD host protocol or guaranteed maximum speed for any arbitrary chip.<ref name="onfi">Open NAND Flash Interface Workgroup, [https://onfi.org/files/ONFI_6_0_Final.pdf ''Open NAND Flash Interface Specification'', revision 6.0 (18 December 2025)]. Consult the exact revision and device data sheet for supported command sequences and timing.</ref>

A host logical write may involve several physical operations: map a new physical page, program it, validate or protect metadata, mark old data obsolete, and later reclaim a block. The physical erasure may happen long after the host reports a logical deletion. Conversely, sudden power loss can interrupt controller metadata updates even when stored charge is nonvolatile.

== Voltage margins, multilevel cells and reliability ==
Single-level cell (SLC) operation represents one bit per cell with two nominal threshold regions. Multi-level operation represents additional bits through more threshold regions (for example, two bits require four logical states). Narrower state separation increases sensitivity to noise, retention drift and disturb, so sensing, encoding, read-retry and error correction become important. Actual performance and endurance depend on device generation, process, temperature, retention age and workload; a universal SLC/MLC/TLC/QLC lifetime figure is not defensible.

Errors arise from wear-induced dielectric damage, charge leakage, program/read disturb, coupling, fabrication defects and controller faults. Experimental work on 2Y-nm planar MLC NAND demonstrates read-disturb-induced threshold shifts under repeated reads; its reported effects and mitigations are device- and workload-specific, not universal constants.<ref name="cai2015">Yu Cai et al., [https://doi.org/10.1109/DSN.2015.49 ''Read Disturb Errors in MLC NAND Flash Memory: Characterization, Mitigation, and Recovery''], DSN 2015; experiments on 2Y-nm planar MLC NAND.</ref> A separate experimental retention study reports systematic shifts in optimal read-reference voltage with retention age in tested MLC devices; this does not establish a universal retention rate.<ref name="cai2018">Yu Cai et al., [https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.02819 ''Experimental Characterization, Optimization, and Recovery of Data Retention Errors in MLC NAND Flash Memory''], 2018 account of HPCA 2015 experimental work.</ref> Raw bit-error rate is not identical to uncorrectable data-loss probability. ECC can correct errors within a specified code and operating regime, but its protection has finite limits. Scrubbing, redundancy, bad-block retirement and power-loss protection address different risks.

== Quantitative capacity and write amplification ==
For a hypothetical NAND geometry with <math>4096</math> data bytes per page, <math>256</math> pages per block and <math>1024</math> usable blocks, nominal user-data area before reserved space and metadata is
<math display="block">4096\times256\times1024=2^{30}\ \mathrm{bytes}=1\ \mathrm{GiB}.</math>
This is an invented geometry, not a specification of a commercial chip. Spare/OOB bytes, bad blocks, controller reservation and formatting change usable capacity.

Write amplification is defined over a chosen observation window as
<math display="block">WA=\frac{\text{physical bytes programmed}}{\text{host bytes written}},</math>
with a consistent accounting boundary. If an illustrative controller programs 12 GiB while receiving 8 GiB of host writes, <math>WA=1.5</math>. The ratio does not by itself determine wear lifetime, since distribution across blocks, temperature, ECC margin, cell technology and workload matter. Counting metadata and background copying changes the measurement boundary. Garbage collection, overprovisioning and write locality affect this ratio.

== Translation, durability and security ==
An FTL maps host logical addresses to physical locations, reconciles block erasure with logical overwrites and tracks valid pages. Wear leveling distributes erase cycles; garbage collection reclaims blocks with obsolete pages; bad-block management retires unreliable regions. These algorithms are controller policies, not physical definitions of flash. Managed NAND devices, SSDs, removable cards and raw chips expose different responsibilities to their hosts.

Durability requires a complete chain: application ordering, filesystem journal or transaction protocol, host flush/FUA semantics where supported, device volatile-cache handling, controller metadata integrity and physical programming completion. Nonvolatility does not guarantee atomic writes, nor does an acknowledged host command universally imply persistence across power failure. Likewise, ordinary file deletion or a logical overwrite does not prove immediate physical erasure of remapped cells. Security claims require interface- and device-specific sanitize/crypto-erase guarantees and threat models.

== Evidence limits and research questions ==
Vendor specifications are normative for the specified device or interface, not empirical distributions of field failure. Accelerated endurance/retention tests require explicit stress conditions and extrapolation models. Cross-device comparisons should control for process generation, temperature, retention time, programmed states, workload, ECC configuration and controller policy. Useful research questions include threshold-voltage distribution evolution, disturb mitigation, FTL crash consistency and lifetime under measured write traces.

